[; reference/shop/delayed-shipment — COMPOSITE feature.
   The point of the reference app: a feature nobody authored the data for.
   It joins `orders` and `shipments` over the shared `order-id` KEY and the
   shared `time` FRAME, and derives an alert noun that exists in neither
   base — the delay is a relationship between two features, so it can only
   live above both.

   W5 (derived-reference existence) is what makes the WIRING honest: the
   names in `uses` and in the derived verb's `[constituents …]` must resolve
   inside the composed grammar. Enable this feature without `orders` and the
   gate refuses it rather than producing a grammar with a dangling join.

   `[from …]` IS CHECKED TOO, as of #840. It is a list of qualified noun
   references, one per string item, and W5 requires each to resolve to a noun
   of the composed grammar — so `[from 'orders/order' 'shipments/shipment']`
   below is load-bearing, not a note. Name a noun in a feature that is not
   enabled and the gate refuses the composite.

   THE JOIN IS COMPUTED BY A DECLARED DERIVER (#865, R8.1/§4.2) — never by
   the engine, and never by a grammar verb. `[from …]` says what
   `delay-alert` is derived FROM; the `detect` deriver (declared in
   shop.xap.cxd's [principals], implemented by detect.cx) reads exactly
   those sources and emits the noun's events as `actor: deriver:detect`.
   No join algebra is committed: the join lives as ordinary CX inside the
   deriver. The join this noun means is:

     orders/order JOIN shipments/shipment
       ON    orders/order.id = shipments/shipment.for-order
       WHERE shipments/shipment.delivered-at IS ABSENT
         AND orders/order.promised-at BEFORE now

   W7 (derived-noun write exclusivity) keeps this the ONLY producer: a
   grammar verb declaring [writes delay-alert] refuses at compose, and a
   runtime whose grammar carries this noun refuses assembly (CXER4875)
   until a deriver is bound.

   N-COMPOSE-2 rides the derived verb. `escalate` invokes
   `shipments/ship`'s neighbourhood of authority through its constituents,
   so the PEP admits it only for a principal holding the CONSTITUENT
   grants — granting the wrapper's own name conveys nothing. That is what
   stops a composite from laundering authority. ]
[feature name=delayed-shipment version="1" kind=composite
 [summary 'Alerts when a shipment has not moved by the time the order was promised.']

 [uses features='orders shipments']

 [frames
  [use frame=time]]
 [keys
  [key name=order-id via=for-order]]

 [nouns
  [noun name=delay-alert derived=true
   [summary 'One order whose promise has passed with no delivery — derived, never sourced.']
   [field name=for-order type=text]
   [field name=customer type=text]
   [field name=promised-at type=instant]
   [field name=shipped-at type=instant]
   [field name=carrier type=text]
   [field name=overdue-by type=duration]
   [from 'orders/order' 'shipments/shipment']]]

 [verbs
  [verb name=review effect=observe
   [summary 'Look at the current delay alerts.']
   [intent [do :review]]
   [reads delay-alert]]
  [verb name=escalate effect=act scope=shared consequence=irreversible
   [summary 'Escalate a delayed shipment: re-dispatch and tell the customer. DERIVED — its authority is its constituents.']
   [intent [do :escalate [for-order]]]
   [reads delay-alert]
   [writes shipment]
   [constituents 'shipments/ship orders/cancel-order']]]

 [; WHERE `raise-alert` WENT (#865, R8.2). This feature used to carry a
    `raise-alert` act verb as the derived noun's hand-rolled producer — the
    workaround the #840 close said stays until derivation was ruled. It was
    ruled: a derived noun is DERIVER-RESERVED. The producer is now the
    `detect` deriver declared in shop.xap.cxd and implemented by detect.cx,
    committing as `actor: deriver:detect` through [$xap:derive]; W7 refuses
    any grammar verb that tries to [writes] this noun again. A manual
    override, if one is ever wanted, is a SOURCE noun the deriver folds
    (listed in [from …]) — attributable input, never a write verb. ]
 [; THE CROSS-FEATURE RULES LIVE HERE, not in the bases. `shipments` may not
    say "ship only after orders/place-order" — that reaches outside its own
    grammar and W4 refuses it (see the note in shipments.feature.cxd). This
    feature MAY say it, because `uses` puts both bases inside its reach.
    That is the composition model's whole shape: features stay independent,
    and the relationships between them are themselves a feature. ]
 [rules
  [rule name=ship-after-place kind=ordering verb=shipments/ship after=orders/place-order
   [statement 'A shipment can only be dispatched for an order that was placed.']]
  [rule name=escalate-after-ship kind=ordering verb=delayed-shipment/escalate after=shipments/ship
   [statement 'A delay can only be escalated after the shipment it concerns was dispatched.']]
  [rule name=alert-needs-promise kind=validity
   [statement 'A delay alert MUST NOT be raised for an order that carries no promised-at.']]]

 [requirements
  [requirement kind=functional as=fulfilment traces=review
   [want 'to see which shipments are overdue against what we promised']
   [so 'I can act before the customer has to ask']
   [acceptance 'delay-alert is present in the composed grammar as a derived noun']]
  [requirement kind=functional as=fulfilment traces=escalate
   [want 'to escalate an overdue shipment']
   [so 'the customer gets the goods or their money back']
   [acceptance 'escalate derives effect=act, scope=shared and consequence=irreversible from its constituents']
   [acceptance 'a principal granted only delayed-shipment/escalate is REFUSED — the denial names the constituent']
   [acceptance 'a principal granted shipments/ship and orders/cancel-order is admitted']]
  [requirement kind=domain level=must traces=escalate
   [statement 'A composite MUST NOT weaken the effect signature it derives from its constituents.']
   [acceptance 'declaring escalate as effect=observe is rejected as a W5-class violation']]]]
